// `.clear()` over the scalar list reps that `.pop()` already serves.
//
// silent-class inventory D1131. `.pop()` runs at i32, string, boolean, ref, f64, f32, i64 and u8;
// `.clear()` runs at only FOUR of those and fails three different ways at the rest:
//
//   f64[] / f32[]  ->  emitProgram: .clear but list type not collected     (loud, clause 2)
//   u8[]           ->  emitProgram: unsupported member-call statement      (loud, one stage EARLIER)
//   i64[]          ->  check-clean INVALID WASM                            (clause 1)
//
// D977 built the scalar ladder for `pop` only, and nothing asked `clear` the same question.
//
// The i64 face is why this is not merely a capability gap: `vl check` returns 0 and the module
// does not validate, so one rep of one method is a soundness violation while its siblings are
// loud and its cousin `.pop()` is fine.
//
// Should print 0 then 0 then 0 then 0.
const a: f64[] = [1.5, 2.5]
a.clear()
print(a.length)

const b: u8[] = "ab".bytes()
b.clear()
print(b.length)

const c: i64[] = [1, 2]
c.clear()
print(c.length)

const d: f32[] = [1.5, 2.5]
d.clear()
print(d.length)
